<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Funj Chronicle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">19th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Katib al-Shuna chronicle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Funj Chronicle is a history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj Sultanate of Sennar</a> and the early Turco-Egyptian period in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, compiled in Arabic in the nineteenth century and attributed in its original form to Ahmad Katib al-Shuna. It survives in several recensions and provides the traditional king lists and much of the narrative of Funj history, though its account of the early centuries rests on late tradition.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The original chronicle was written by Shaykh Aḥmad, known as Kātib al-Shūna, who began it before the conquest of the Funj in 1820 and carried it to 1838. Two versions circulated, an early draft and a polished text with some fifteen passages removed. Shaykh Aḥmad al-Ḥājj Muḥammad Janqāl continued it and inserted passages of his own. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-zubayr-rahma-mansur">Al-Zubayr</a> wad Ḍawwah extended it to 1863 and added material on medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> taken from Ibn Sulaym al-Aswānī by way of al-Maqrīzī. A final recension by Ibrāhīm ʿAbd al-Dāfiʿ cut most of those additions and, with a notice from al-Amīn Muḥammad al-Ḍarīr, brought the chronicle to 1871.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The author admits that his sources are mostly anecdotes, often contradictory, and for later years family stories and his own memory. His written sources were a king list, distinct from the one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce">James Bruce</a> acquired in 1772, and the Ṭabaqāt of Wad Ḍayfallāh, a biographical dictionary of Sudanese holy men. The historians O'Fahey and Spaulding called the early part a king list with some commentary added. The narrative grows fuller from the reign of Bādī IV (1724–1762), and from 1788 or 1789 it drops the reign-by-reign structure, since by then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamaj">Hamaj</a> held power and the Funj kingship had become customary.</p><p>Its horizon is narrow, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> and the capital Sinnār, and regions such as the lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> are only occasionally mentioned. After the conquest it is arranged by the terms of the governors-general.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Makkī Shubayka published the original recension at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> in 1947 as Taʾrīkh mulūk al-Sūdān, and the final recension appeared at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1963 as Makhṭūṭat Kātib al-Shūna, edited by Al-Shater Bossely Abdul Jalil. Harold MacMichael made a summary English translation in 1922. Peter Holt's The Sudan of the Three Niles (1999) was the first complete English translation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Funj Chronicle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the <b>Funj Chronicle</b> and later tradition, their leader Amara Dunqas founded Sennar in 1504, a date accepted by most historians though it rests on late sources.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
